// Property 1:
// This property checks the probability that the system eventually reaches a state
// where cybersickness level is moderate (cybersickness_level = 1).
//
// Source Claim from Document:
// Claim #1: "If the virtual object in the field of view is moving at a speed greater than X2 m/s,
// the object’s motion will cause the SSQ score to exceed S1 for U1% of operators with W1% probability."
//
// Mapping:
// Concept from Doc                       -> Mapping in Model
// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Virtual object moving fast             -> state_sys_opticflow < state_opticflow_threshold
// Causes SSQ score to exceed S1         -> cybersickness_level = 1 (moderate cybersickness)
// W1% probability of sickness            -> Computed by PRISM as: P=? [ F cybersickness_level = 1 ]
//
// In the model:
// - The only way cybersickness_level = 1 is triggered is if:
//     1. Optic flow is abnormal → increases state_opticflow_counter
//     2. That increases c3x3 (c3 * state_opticflow_counter)
//     3. Which contributes to sum = c1x1 + c2x2 + c3x3
//     4. If sum is between ssq_low and ssq_medium, then cybersickness_level becomes 1
//
// The property captures the end result of an optic flow-induced cybersickness scenario.

P>=0.5 [ (state_sys_opticflow < state_opticflow_threshold) U cybersickness_level = 1 ]

// This property states that with at least 50% probability, the system will eventually reach a state where cybersickness_level = 1 (i.e., moderate cybersickness), and until that point, the optic flow remains below the defined threshold (state_sys_opticflow < state_opticflow_threshold) at every step along the path.


// Property 2:
// This property checks the probability that the system avoids high cybersickness level 
// (i.e., SSQ score does not exceed S2) assuming none of the triggering conditions are active.
// We use this to confirm that the system remains in either no cybersickness (0) or moderate (1),
// and never reaches high severity (2), when no latency or optic flow violations are detected.
//
// Source Guarantee from Document:
// "# sickness guarantee: If the assumptions are true AND none of the claims in the model are met,
// then the user’s SSQ (cybersickness) score will not exceed S1 with W4% probability."
//
// Mapping:
// | Concept from Doc                          | Mapping in Model                                               |
// |-------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------|
// | "None of the claims met"                  | Latency and opticflow values are within safe thresholds        |
// | "SSQ score will not exceed S1"            | cybersickness_level ≠ 2 (i.e., stays at 0 or 1)                |
// | "Probability of remaining below S1"       | Computed as: P=? [ G (cybersickness_level < 2) ]               |
//
// Notes:
// - "None of the claims met" means we assume the following conditions hold true:
//     - state_io_input_link_systemdata_sys_latency ≤ state_latency_threshold
//     - state_sys_opticflow ≥ state_opticflow_threshold
//   which prevent proposal of violation-handling operators in hardware phase.
// - cybersickness_level is updated in cybersickness APPLY phase based on sum of weights.
// - This property checks that even after all transitions, the system avoids cybersickness_level = 2.
//
// Model Checking Query:
// Now:
// P>=0.9 [ G (
//  state_io_input_link_systemdata_sys_latency <= state_latency_threshold &
//  state_sys_opticflow >= state_opticflow_threshold
//  => cybersickness_level < 2
// ) ]

// But Prism can’t do implication directly. However, we can apply DeMorgan’s law to convert it into not and or statements (which Prism supports):

P>=0.9 [ G (   !(state_io_input_link_systemdata_sys_latency <= state_latency_threshold) |   !(state_sys_opticflow >= state_opticflow_threshold) |   cybersickness_level < 2 ) ] 

// This property states that with at least 90% probability, whenever both latency and optic flow remain within safe bounds, the system will stay in a state where cybersickness_level remains less than 2 — that is, it never enters a state of high cybersickness.
